## Findings

[HIGH] Cloud quote retrieval disables TLS certificate validation
File: `D:\My Drive\Blue AI\Hardware_Rather_Empty\Entropy\src\main.cpp:84-91`

Issue: The HTTPS client explicitly accepts any certificate:

```cpp
WiFiClientSecure cli;
cli.setInsecure();
...
if (!http.begin(cli, API_URL)) return false;
```

Failure scenario: an attacker controlling or impersonating a Wi-Fi access point, DNS resolution, or an on-path network connection presents any TLS certificate for the Cloudflare hostname. The device accepts it and renders attacker-supplied quote data. The existing HTTPS URL and `HTTPClient` do not protect this flow because `setInsecure()` turns off hostname/certificate authentication; redirect-following further broadens the trusted response origin.

Fix: validate the Cloudflare Worker certificate chain with an appropriate CA bundle, or pin the Worker/public-key certificate and rotate it deliberately. Treat the resulting payload as untrusted even after TLS validation.

[HIGH] Low-pool prefetch blocks the main loop immediately after beginning an animation
File: `D:\My Drive\Blue AI\Hardware_Rather_Empty\Entropy\src\main.cpp:580-585`, `84-101`, `606-620`

Issue: `rollQuote()` starts the animation via `loadQuote()`, then synchronously calls `fetchQuotes()` whenever the pool contains fewer than four entries. `fetchQuotes()` performs a blocking HTTP GET, permits up to a 10-second socket timeout, reads the complete response, and parses it on the same Arduino loop task.

```cpp
loadQuote(curQuote, true, true);

if (quotes.size() < 4 && WiFi.status() == WL_CONNECTED) {
  fetchQuotes();
}
```

Failure scenario: with one to three cached quotes, a user presses Roll while the Worker is slow or unreachable. Frame 0 has been drawn, but the synchronous fetch prevents `stepAnimation()` and button polling from running until the request returns or times out. The cipher animation visibly freezes and the controls become unresponsive for up to at least the configured timeout; a server that continues producing response data can extend this further during `getString()`.

Fix: defer prefetch until the animation is complete and the UI is idle, or run the network operation in a separate FreeRTOS task with a bounded handoff queue. Do not mutate UI state from that task; only atomically publish validated, bounded quote objects.

[MEDIUM] Pool trimming invalidates `curQuote` while the current pages remain active
File: `D:\My Drive\Blue AI\Hardware_Rather_Empty\Entropy\src\main.cpp:133-141`, `450-456`, `582-585`, `780-788`

Issue: `fetchQuotes()` erases entries from `quotes` without preserving or adjusting the index of the quote currently represented by `pages`/the display.

```cpp
if (added > 0 && !quotes.empty() && quotes[0].idx == "!") {
  quotes.erase(quotes.begin());
}

while (quotes.size() > 20) {
  quotes.erase(quotes.begin());
}
```

Failure scenario: boot offline, showing the standby quote at `curQuote == 0`; then Wi-Fi becomes available and the idle refresh receives a cloud quote. The standby element is erased, but `pages` still contains standby text while later `finishAnimation()`, a page change, or a redraw reads header/footer metadata from `quotes[curQuote]`, which is now the cloud quote. At the 20-entry cap, each subsequent successful refresh similarly shifts or removes the displayed element. The screen can therefore combine text from one quote with metadata/tags from another and selection logic no longer excludes the quote actually on screen.

Fix: retain a stable identifier or an owned `activeQuote` for the displayed content, and resolve its current pool position after mutations. Alternatively, defer removal/eviction until selecting the next quote and never evict the active entry; adjust `curQuote` whenever an earlier element is erased.

[MEDIUM] External response size and retained field sizes are unbounded before the 20-item cap
File: `D:\My Drive\Blue AI\Hardware_Rather_Empty\Entropy\src\main.cpp:98-140`

Issue: The code copies the entire HTTP entity into a `String`, parses it into a dynamic ArduinoJson document, copies all quote fields/tags into heap-backed objects, and only then limits the number of retained quotes. There is no Content-Length limit, streaming cap, JSON nesting/filter constraint, per-field length limit, tag-count limit, or maximum number of array elements processed.

```cpp
String body = http.getString();
...
JsonDocument doc;
if (deserializeJson(doc, body)) return false;
...
quotes.push_back(q);
...
while (quotes.size() > 20) {
  quotes.erase(quotes.begin());
}
```

Failure scenario: a malformed Worker deployment, proxy error page, or the unauthenticated network path above returns a large JSON array or a single record with a very large `content`/`tags` field. The ESP32-C3 must allocate the complete response, parsed document, copied strings, and temporary vector entries before the late cap executes. Heap exhaustion can reset the device or leave it unable to render/fetch; repeated fetches also increase fragmentation pressure.

Fix: reject oversized `Content-Length` values, impose a hard streamed-byte limit for chunked responses, deserialize from the stream with restrictive filters/nesting limits, validate maximum field/tag/array sizes before copying, and bound the pool during parsing rather than afterward.

## Review Summary

| Severity | Count | Status |
|----------|-------|--------|
| CRITICAL | 0 | pass |
| HIGH | 2 | warn |
| MEDIUM | 2 | info |
| LOW | 0 | pass |

Partition assessment: `D:\My Drive\Blue AI\Hardware_Rather_Empty\Entropy\platformio.ini:12` selects the standard `huge_app.csv`; the generated table has a 3 MiB single app partition, no OTA application slot, plus unused SPIFFS/coredump partitions. It does not itself persist the RAM quote pool, so I found no correctness or security finding in the partition change.

Verdict: WARNING — resolve the two HIGH issues before merge. No files were modified.

